<?xml version="1.0" encoding="windows-1252"?>
<SEC xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="http://si.ksc.nasa.gov/sidownloads/xml/specsintactSEC.xsd">
<MTA NAME="SUBFORMAT" CONTENT="NEW"/>
<MTA NAME="AUTONUMBER" CONTENT="TRUE"/>
<HDR><AST/>
<HL4>UNIFIED FACILITIES GUIDE SPECIFICATIONS</HL4><BRK/>
<AST/><BRK/></HDR>
<BRK/>
<SCN>SECTION 31 23 19</SCN><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<STL>CONSTRUCTION DEWATERING</STL><BRK/>
<DTE>04/26</DTE><BRK/>
<PRT>
<TTL>PART 1 GENERAL</TTL><BRK/>
<SPT>
<TTL>DESCRIPTION</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>This section establishes performance criteria and requirements for the following four construction dewatering systems:</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a."><BLD>Dry Dock Mass Excavation Dewatering System</BLD> - Dewatering within the Dry Dock seepage cutoff wall perimeter (upland and offshore sections) to lower and maintain the water table a minimum of 3 feet below the working ground surface and prevent soil migration or basal instability.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b."><BLD>Temporary Cofferdam Interior Level Control System</BLD> - Dewatering within the temporary cofferdam fill material to maintain the interior water level to a maximum elevation of 75 feet and maintain the maximum allowable water level differential specified in Drawing Set 1215 - Double Wall Cofferdam.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c."><BLD>Cofferdam Capture Area Unwatering System</BLD> - Pumps, piping, and appurtenances to remove surface water from the cofferdam capture area and the existing Dry Dock 3 (DD3). This system is activated following completion of the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall and offshore Double Wall cofferdam.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d."><BLD>Upland Concrete Shoring Wall Dewatering System</BLD> - Dewatering wells on the exterior of the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall to maintain the water table at or below elevation 95 feet (PSNS datum) and achieve the hydrostatic pressure criteria in paragraph 1.5.3.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
<TXT>Dewatering for ancillary structures that are not included in above four systems are not addressed in this section, see Section <SRF>31 23 00.00 20</SRF> , EXCAVATION AND FILL. Stormwater management activities are not included herein, see Section <SRF>01 57 19</SRF> , TEMPORARY ENVIRONMENTAL CONTROLS.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<TXT>This section specifies the design, installation, operation, maintenance, monitoring, and decommissioning requirements of temporary dewatering and groundwater control systems required to lower and control groundwater levels and hydrostatic pressures for the four dewatering systems described above to permit excavation, construction, and backfilling to be completed in the dry for the construction of the P454 Multi-Mission Dry Dock (M2D2).</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<TXT>Work specified herein includes control of surface water, groundwater, tidal seepage, precipitation, and water generated from construction activities entering the subsurface within the influence of the four dewatering systems from all construction and work areas.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<TXT>Discharge all extracted water from dewatering systems to a designated pre-treatment system. Water treatment and discharge compliance requirements are specified in Section <SRF>01 57 19</SRF> , TEMPORARY ENVIRONMENTAL CONTROLS. Treatment systems are not within the scope of this section.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<TXT>Follow Instrumentation and performance monitoring requirements, including observation wells, piezometers, settlement monuments, and displacement monitoring, in accordance with Section <SRF>33 51 43</SRF> , INSTRUMENTATION AND PERFORMANCE MONITORING OF STRUCTURES.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<TXT>Contractor is responsible for reviewing and complying with all project contract documents.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<SPT>
<TTL>Multi-Contract Execution</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>M2D2 construction is executed under three construction contracts. Construction dewatering efforts are completed primarily under Contract 2; however, some elements - including the Dry Dock Mass Excavation Dewatering System and Temporary Cofferdam Interior Level Control System - require transfer between Contract 2 and Contract 3. Contractors executing work under Contract 2 and Contract 3 are to adhere to the dewatering requirements in this section.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<TXT>Some project elements in Contract 1 require construction dewatering. Dewatering for these components is expected to be temporary. Determine design, means, methods, and sequencing as outlined in Section <SRF>31 23 00.00 20</SRF> , Excavation and Fill. Manage all water to comply with applicable site and environmental permits as outlined in Section <SRF>01 57 19</SRF> , TEMPORARY ENVIRONMENTAL CONTROLS, and all other contract documents.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>MEASUREMENT AND PAYMENT</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Measurement and payment for dewatering is in accordance with Section <SRF>01 20 00</SRF> , PRICE AND PAYMENT PROCEDURES.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>DEFINITIONS</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Construction Dewatering Discharge Endpoint: The pre-treatment system as defined in Section <SRF>01 57 19</SRF> , TEMPORARY ENVIRONMENTAL CONTROLS.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Dewatering System: The complete assembly of all assets required to achieve and maintain in-the-dry conditions or the prescribed water level control objectives for a given system including, but not limited to, wells, wellpoints, eductors, sumps, trenches, headers, pumps, piping, power supply, controls, instrumentation, and associated appurtenances.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Dewatering Team: Qualified personnel assigned to operate, maintain, and carry out dewatering system operations and maintenance.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Dry Dock: The area within the footprint of Dry Dock construction.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">In-the-Dry: A condition where the groundwater level has been lowered to at least 3 feet below the lowest level of the excavation and the subgrade is stable with no ponding water, mud, or muck. The subgrade supports construction equipment without rutting or disturbance and supports placement and compaction of fill material or placement of concrete or structural foundations.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">M2D2: The P454 Multi-Mission Dry Dock project site.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="g.">PSNS: Puget Sound Naval Shipyard.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="h.">Qualified Personnel: A person or persons who have the experience and training required to operate and maintain the dewatering systems, including completion of the field training program specified in paragraph 3.4.3.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="i.">Tidal Lag: The delayed response of groundwater levels within or behind a cofferdam or cutoff wall to tidal fluctuations in adjacent water bodies.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>DESIGN AND PERFORMANCE RESPONSIBILITY</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Design and performance of all dewatering systems identified in paragraph 1.1 are the sole responsibility of the Contractor. This responsibility extends to all damages to Government property, existing structures, utilities, marine infrastructure, adjacent properties, completed Work, and Work in progress resulting from dewatering operations.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Review and approval of submittals by the Contracting Officer does not relieve the Contractor of responsibility for the design, performance, and safety of the dewatering systems.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">The Geotechnical Data Report (GDR) (WSM 2026x) provides soil and groundwater data for this project. Use this information to design dewatering systems.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">The means and methods of controlling groundwater, other water that enters the subsurface, and the cofferdam capture area unwatering are determined by the Contractor with Contracting Officer approval. Design, equipment selection and arrangement, location, performance, monitoring, and depth of any system(s) selected to accomplish the work are the Contractor's responsibility.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>DESIGN CRITERIA</TTL><BRK/>
<SPT>
<TTL>Dry Dock Mass Excavation Dewatering System Performance Criteria</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Design the Dry Dock Mass Excavation Dewatering System to:</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Lower and maintain the water table within the Dry Dock seepage cutoff wall perimeter to a minimum of 3 feet below the working ground surface, in advance of excavation, at all times.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Maintain stability of the sides and bottom of the excavation. Prevent basal instability, loss of fines, piping, boils, quick conditions, and soil migration.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Relieve artesian and sub-artesian pressures to prevent basal heave or uplift of the excavation bottom.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Prevent softening of foundation soils.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Maintain a dry, stable subgrade for construction operations.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Prevent damage to adjacent structures, utilities, marine infrastructure, completed Work, and adjacent property due to settlement, loss of ground, or other groundwater-related effects.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="g.">Account for tidal fluctuations, tidal lag through the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall and Double Wall cofferdam systems, storm surge, and seasonal groundwater variation in system capacity sizing.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="h.">Demonstrate through numerical groundwater flow modeling that the system design maintains an upward hydraulic gradient less than 1.0 at all structural features and at the base of excavation. Provide a minimum factor of safety against basal instability (including, but not limited to internal erosion), supported by industry-standard critical hydraulic gradient calculations. Alternative factors of safety for specific locations require Contracting Officer approval.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="i.">Define water level thresholds that maintain hydraulic gradients at or below the maximum allowable values defined under item (h).</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="j.">Systems must be installed to allow for site work to continue uninterrupted and must have sufficient capacity to accomplish this desired result, allowing for normal variation in groundwater levels, soil properties, precipitation, system efficiencies, as-constructed conditions compared to as-designed conditions, and component downtime.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Temporary Cofferdam Interior Level Control System Performance Criteria</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Design the Temporary Cofferdam Interior Level Control System to:</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Lower and maintain the interior water level of the Temporary Cofferdam to a maximum elevation of 75 feet (PSNS datum).</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Maintain the maximum allowable water level differential between the interior of the Temporary Cofferdam and both exterior sides, as stated in the Double Wall Cofferdam contract documents.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Demonstrate through numerical groundwater flow modeling calculations that the system design maintains an upward hydraulic gradient less than 1.0 at all structural features and at the interface between native soils and overlying temporary cofferdam fill material. Provide a minimum factor of safety against basal instability (including, but not limited to internal erosion), supported by industry-standard critical hydraulic gradient calculations. Alternative gradient values for specific locations require Contracting Officer approval.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Define water level thresholds that maintain hydraulic gradients at or below the maximum allowable values defined under item (c).</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Meet the soil migration, basal stability, and foundation soil criteria of paragraph 1.5.1(b)(c)(d).</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Upland Concrete Shoring Wall Dewatering System Performance Criteria</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Design the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall Dewatering System to:</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Maintain the water table on the exterior of the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall such that zero hydrostatic pressure acts against the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall from elevation 95 feet (PSNS datum) to the top elevation of the shoring wall.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Facilitate the installation of tiebacks through the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall by reducing the hydrostatic pressure acting against the tieback locations during installation, if necessary.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Maintain structural stability requirements for the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall until minimum backfill conditions are met as stated in the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall contract documents.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Cofferdam Capture Area Unwatering System Performance Criteria</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Design the Cofferdam Capture Area Unwatering System to remove surface water from the Cofferdam Capture area, including the DD3 area, following completion and Contracting Officer approval of the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall and offshore Double Wall cofferdam installation. Determine pumping schedule and discharge location in compliance with all applicable permits and treatment system limitations as outlined in Section <SRF>01 57 19</SRF> , TEMPORARY ENVIRONMENTAL CONTROLS.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Ancillary Dewatering Systems Performance Criteria</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Design ancillary dewatering systems as temporary systems to support construction of the structures identified in paragraph 1.1.2. Determine design, means, methods, and sequencing. Specific requirements are in Section <SRF>31 23 00.00 20</SRF> , EXCAVATION AND FILL.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Numerical Groundwater Modeling</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Develop a numerical three-dimensional groundwater flow model for use in designing the Dry Dock Mass Excavation Dewatering System, the Temporary Cofferdam Interior Level Control System, and the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall Dewatering System. Use the model to:</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Determine the final number, locations, depths, and pumping rates of dewatering assets including, but not limited to, dewatering wells, wellpoints, interceptor trenches, and eductor wells.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Perform sensitivity analyses on hydraulic conductivity, recharge, cofferdam/shoring wall seepage rates, and cofferdam/shoring well cutoff depths.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Define water level thresholds that maintain hydraulic gradients at or below the maximum allowable values as stated in paragraph 1.5.1(h) and paragraph 1.5.2(c).</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Support prove-out testing evaluation and subsequent model updates using data collected during prove-out testing.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
<TXT>Following well development, perform pumping tests to allow comparison to and further calibration of the numerical groundwater model and inform additional well installations if necessary.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Dewatering and Groundwater Control Plan</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Submit a <SUB>Dewatering and Groundwater Control Plan</SUB> , design, and calculations a minimum of 30 calendar days prior to commencement of dewatering. Address each of the four systems identified in paragraph 1.1 separately. Include, at a minimum:</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Written description and shop drawings of each system layout, including types, locations, dimensions, depths, and relationships of all system components (wells, wellpoints, eductors, sumps, trenches. headers, pumps, piping, controls, discharge lines).</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Design methodology and calculations demonstrating performance, including hydraulic analysis, numerical three-dimensional groundwater flow model results, well screen and filter sand sizing, pump selection, and estimated transient and steady-state discharge rates. Incorporate the Navy-provided pumping test data included in the Request for Proposal documents.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Analysis of tidal influence on groundwater levels within the excavation, including tidal lag through the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall and Double Wall cofferdam systems, and demonstration that each system maintains its performance criteria throughout the full tidal cycle and during 50-year storm surge events.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Description of the interface between each dewatering system and:</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="d.(1)">The Upland Concrete Shoring Wall for the onshore portion.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="d.(2)">The Double Wall cofferdam for the offshore portion.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="d.(3)">Include assumed residual seepage rates through and beneath each cutoff system.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Sequence of installation relative to excavation stages and coordination between the three construction contracts.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Standby equipment and backup power supply details, including automatic switchover provisions.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="g.">Emergency and contingency procedures for system failure, power loss, flood events. storm events, and tidal surge.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="h.">Conveyance design to the pre-treatment system, including metering and sampling ports.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="i.">Hydraulic gradient calculations and water level threshold definitions demonstrate compliance with paragraphs 1.5.1(h) and 1.5.2(c) and the applicable minimum factor of safety.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="j.">Basis of design for each individual dewatering system.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="k.">Functional descriptions for the Dry Dock Mass Excavation Dewatering System, Upland Concrete Shoring Wall Dewatering System, and Temporary Cofferdam Level Control System.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="l.">Process flow diagrams, process and instrumentation diagrams (P&ID), equipment/piping arrangements and elevations, civil and mechanical details, electrical one-lines, cable/conduit schedules, electrical power plans, instrumentation schedules/layouts, and other drawings required for complete dewatering systems, stamped by a Washington-licensed Professional Engineer.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Hold Points</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Do not pass the following hold points without Contracting Officer verification:</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Hold Point No. 1: Do not excavate below 3 feet above the groundwater level until the applicable dewatering system has completed prove-out testing in accordance with paragraph 3.3.4 and the Contracting Officer has verified that performance criteria have been achieved.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Hold Point No. 2: Do not commence decommissioning of any dewatering system until the Contracting Officer has verified that the structure and backfill are complete to an elevation and weight sufficient to resist hydrostatic uplift against flotation at the maximum anticipated groundwater level.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>PERMITS</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Meet the requirements of all applicable project permits listed in Section <SRF>01 57 19</SRF> -Temporary Environmental Controls and other contract documents</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>SUBMITTALS</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Government approval is required for submittals with a "G" classification. Submittals not having a "G" classification are for Contractor Quality Control approval. Submit the following in accordance with Section <SRF>01 33 00</SRF> , SUBMITTAL PROCEDURES:</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<LST><SUB>SD-01 Preconstruction Submittals</SUB></LST><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<ITM><SUB>Dewatering and Groundwater Control Plan</SUB> ; <SUB>G</SUB></ITM><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<ITM><SUB>Dewatering System Design and Calculations</SUB> ; <SUB>G</SUB></ITM><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<ITM><SUB>Flood and Storm Contingency Plan</SUB> ; <SUB>G</SUB></ITM><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<ITM><SUB>Dewatering Systems Prove-Out Plan</SUB> ; <SUB>G</SUB></ITM><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<LST><SUB>SD-02 Shop Drawings</SUB></LST><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<ITM><SUB>Layout figures for each dewatering system, observation wells, and discharge lines relative to existing and future structures, utilities, and roadways</SUB> ; <SUB>G</SUB></ITM><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<ITM><SUB>Detailed drawings of each dewatering system, including process flow diagrams, P&IDs, equipment/piping arrangements and elevations, civil and mechanical details, electrical one-lines, cable/conduit schedules, electrical power plans, instrumentation schedules/layouts, stamped by a Washington-licensed Professional Engineer</SUB> ; <SUB>G</SUB></ITM><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<LST><SUB>SD-03 Product Data</SUB></LST><BRK/>
<BRK/>
