<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>QUALITY CONTROL</TTL><BRK/>
<SPT>
<TTL>Qualifications</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Dewatering Contractor: Provide evidence of minimum 10 years' experience in the construction of Dewatering Systems and piping systems of similar magnitude and complexity.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">System Designer: Provide evidence of minimum 10 years' experience in designing, constructing, and operating construction dewatering and piping systems of similar magnitude and complexity. Provide a Licensed Professional Engineer (PE) in the State of Washington.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Well Designer: Provide evidence of minimum 10 years' experience in designing, installing, and operating dewatering wells. Provide a Licensed Geologist or Professional Engineer in the State of Washington.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Well Driller: Provide evidence of minimum 5 years' experience. Provide a licensed contractor in the State of Washington. Ensure on-site drilling personnel are OSHA Hazardous Waste trained in accordance with 29 CFR 1910.120.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Surveyor: Provide a Licensed Professional Land Surveyor in the State of Washington.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Professional Engineer Certification</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Submit <SUB>PE Certification</SUB> on the form specified in Section <SRF>01 33 00</SRF> , SUBMITTAL PROCEDURES. Provide PE certification that each dewatering system design meets the applicable performance criteria. The certifying PE is responsible for its execution.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Pre-Installation Meeting</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Hold a pre-installation meeting to outline each system's installation schedule and sequence. Ensure that all involved subcontractors and subconsultants are represented. Furnish the date and time of the meeting to the Contracting Officer for approval.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>HEALTH AND SAFETY REQUIREMENTS</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Refer to Section <SRF>01 35 26</SRF> , GOVERNMENTAL SAFETY REQUIREMENTS.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
</PRT>
<PRT>
<TTL>PART 2 PRODUCTS</TTL><BRK/>
<SPT>
<TTL>GENERAL</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Provide materials and equipment of size, capacity, and type to:</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Achieve and maintain the performance criteria for each dewatering system specified in paragraph 1.5.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Collect and remove groundwater, tidal seepage, precipitation, and surface water runoff from the excavation to maintain dry and stable working surfaces.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Convey extracted water to the pre-treatment system.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Withstand exposure to saltwater or brackish groundwater conditions and be compatible with known contaminants of concern at the site (see Division 01 Specifications for list of contaminants). All wetted components are to be corrosion-resistant.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Keep replacement parts on site to allow for timely equipment repair and replacement.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>DEWATERING WELLS</TTL><BRK/>
<SPT>
<TTL>Dry Dock Mass Excavation Dewatering System Wells</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Install dewatering wells in the upland and offshore sections of the Dry Dock construction area and convey water to the pretreatment system. A conceptual well network layout is included on Drawing Sheets 2380-D101 through 2380-D103. Final number of dewatering wells, well locations, pipe sizing, pipe routing, power supply, equipment selection, design and installation are the Contractor's responsibility.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Design well screen slot sizes, screen material, sand pack material and sizing based on the filter sand and formation gradation. Screen material is to be compatible with anticipated saltwater or brackish conditions.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Filter sand: clean, washed, uniformly graded silica sand. Design filter sand gradation based on the formation grain-size distribution.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Annular seal material: granular or pelletized bentonite conforming to manufacturer's specifications. Use neat cement grout or cement-bentonite grout for the upper seal and surface completion.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Well diameters must accommodate submersible pumps capable of producing the Contractor-estimated dewatering flow rates for each well.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Protect each dewatering well via vaults, barriers, bollards, or additional protective measures to prevent damage and operational interruptions.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Temporary Cofferdam Interior Level Control Wells</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">A conceptual well network layout is included on Drawing Sheets 2385-D101 through 2385-D104. Final design is the Contractor's responsibility.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Install level control dewatering wells in locations that allow for continuous dewatering system access and operation.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">All applicable requirements of paragraph 2.2.1 apply unless otherwise approved by the Contracting Officer.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Upland Concrete Shoring Wall Dewatering Wells</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">A conceptual well network layout is included in Drawing Sheets 2380-D101 through 2380-D104. Final design is the Contractor's responsibility.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Include dewatering wells located on the exterior of the Deep Soil Mixing (DSM) zone and in the gap between the DSM and the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall to achieve the dewatering requirements from Section 1.5.3.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Select dewatering well type (e.g., wellpoint, deep well, ejector well) based on site conditions and performance criteria.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">All applicable requirements of paragraph 2.2.1 apply unless otherwise approved by the Contracting Officer.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>PUMPS AND POWER SUPPLY</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Provide pumps with capacity to achieve and maintain the performance criteria for each system under all anticipated conditions, including peak tidal seepage and storm events.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Equip each dewatering well pump with a variable frequency drive (VFD) controller interfaced with a centralized programmable logic controller (PLC). Program the PLC so VFDs for each individual pump are automatically adjusted based on information relayed from level monitoring equipment. VFDs are to control pump speed based on either groundwater elevation or groundwater extraction flow rate, as set by the operator.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Equip each pump's individual conveyance pipe with an inline check valve, appropriately sized flow meter connected to the PLC, and isolation valve prior to connection to the main header pipe.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Equip each dewatering well with a level transducer connected to the PLC to allow for real-time control and performance monitoring. Transducers are to send continuous level data to the PLC, where the system logic determines the pump state (on/off) and speed to maintain the required piezometric level.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Stock backup pumps on site to ensure that each dewatering system operates with minimal and acceptable interruption and meets dewatering objectives at all times. Obtain Contracting Officer approval of backup pump quantities.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Provide a primary power source and a backup power source (e.g., backup generators) capable of continuous operation of all dewatering systems. The backup source is to allow maintenance and repair on the primary source without system interruption. Provide automatic startup and automatic transfer switch in the event of primary power failure. Test backup power weekly to confirm operability.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="g.">Install pumps, generators, and switchgear at elevations or in enclosures that protect them from flooding, storm surge, and wave action to the 50-year flood elevation plus 3 feet of freeboard. Install all electrical systems in accordance with federal, state, and local code and other project requirements.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="h.">Determine the need for system insulation during cold weather periods to prevent freezing of pumps, piping, and system appurtenance..</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>SYSTEMS CONTROLS, INSTRUMENTATION, AND ALARMS</TTL><BRK/>
<SPT>
<TTL>Centralized Control System</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Install a centralized PLC system (which may be comprised of multiple panels) to allow for complete system operation, alarm/fault monitoring, and data collection for all dewatering systems.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">The PLC system is to monitor and record water levels, individual well and system flow rates, total dewatering system flow, pre-treatment system status, and pump and piping pressures.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">The PLC system is to allow control of VFDs to adjust pump flow rates and maintain desired drawdown, groundwater elevation, flow rate, and pre-treatment system water level.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">System controls are to allow for manual/off/automatic operational control options for each pump.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Provide real-time flowrate, totalized flow, and operational pressures for each pump accessible on the human machine interface (HMI), as well as additional pressure and flow readings from other points in the system. Provide real-time readouts from groundwater level monitoring devices on the HMI.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Provide live PLC system data accessible to the Contractor and Contracting Officer. Include data logging capabilities.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="g.">Time-stamp, record, and store all collected data and alarms for the duration of the project.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="h.">House the control panel in material appropriate for the local environment.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="i.">Ensure motor controls conform to <RID>NEMA ICS 1</RID> and enclosures for power and control panels conform to <RID>NEMA ICS 6</RID> .</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Dewatering System Instrumentation</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Provide each dewatering well with an appropriately sized and grounded flow meter capable of measuring, recording, and transmitting instantaneous and totalized flow. Flow meters are to communicate with the central PLC.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Provide flow meters at a minimum of 4 points along each main header pipe to allow for system performance evaluation and support identification of system problems.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Install a flowmeter on the header pipe entering the pre-treatment system to allow for collection of total system flow rate and totalized flow.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Flow meter accuracy: plus or minus 5 percent of reading. Install flow meters in locations readily accessible for reading and maintenance.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Provide pressure gauges connected to conveyance pipe for each individual dewatering well pump and along the header pipe at intervals defined by the Contractor and approved by the Contracting Officer. Provide precision type gauges with bronze Bourdon tube and phenolic case and accuracy of plus or minus 1/2 percent conforming to <RID>ASME B40.100</RID> .</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Provide pressure transmitters on conveyance pipe for each individual dewatering well pump and along the header pipe at intervals defined by the Contractor and approved by the Contracting Officer for real-time performance monitoring and alarm callouts.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>System Alarms</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Configure the system to include alarms on pumps, level controls, flow meters, pressure sensors, and transducers throughout the system, including at a minimum:</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="a.(1)">Pump/VFD operational fault</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="a.(2)">Pump run status</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="a.(3)">Pumping well transducer fault; water level low-low/low/high/high-high</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="a.(4)">Flow meter low/high flow; flow meter fault</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="a.(5)">Pressure sensor low/high pressure; pressure sensor fault</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="a.(6)">Monitoring transducer fault; water level low-low/low/high/high-high</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">In the event of a system alarm, an audible alarm is to alert the system operator of an upset condition. Equip the system with a telemetry system that alerts operators and the Contracting Officer via text message, phone call, or email.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Program system alarms for conditions that signal operators that the system or system components are not performing as designed and to prevent damage to equipment, piping, tanks, and the Dry Dock.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Include system operations and alarm descriptions in the functional description submittals.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Dewatering Effectiveness Monitoring</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Measure pore water pressure next to critical structures using grout-in-place vibrating wire piezometers as required in Section <SRF>33 51 43</SRF> , INSTRUMENTATION AND PERFORMANCE MONITORING OF STRUCTURES..</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Install monitoring wells with transducers to monitor performance of each dewatering system. transducer layout must verify that the required vertical and lateral extents of dewatering and pore water pressure reduction are met.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Place groundwater level monitoring instrumentation away from dewatering wells in locations that demonstrate the effectiveness of each dewatering system.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Coordinate the <SUB>Instrumentation Monitoring Plan</SUB> with the overall project instrumentation and monitoring plan and conform with Section <SRF>33 51 43</SRF> , INSTRUMENTATION AND PERFORMANCE MONITORING OF STRUCTURES.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>PIPING AND CONVEYANCE</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Terminate the main header pipe at the pre-treatment system.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Design, size, and install the header pipe to allow for flow in both directions around the perimeter of the construction area to allow the system to remain operational during repairs or maintenance. A redundant header pipe is also acceptable.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Fit the header pipe with isolation valves to facilitate flow control, repairs, cleanouts, maintenance, monitoring, and cleaning.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Instrument the header pipe with flow meters and pressure sensors connected to the PLC at a minimum of 4 points along the header length.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Provide expansion joints (bellows, braided, or expansion loops) at locations along the main header to ensure that pipe expansion and contraction due to temperature variations do not cause damage to the piping system.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Size discharge piping to convey peak dewatering flows without surcharging.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="g.">Provide piping of appropriate size and suitable material that can withstand maximum process flow pressures as determined by the Contractor. Route and support piping to avoid interference with construction operations, traffic, and utilities. All connections are to be leak-free.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="h.">Provide check valves on each conveyance pipe prior to reaching the main header pipe.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
<SPT>
<TTL>System Sampling Requirements</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Provide sample ports on all conveyance piping, at a minimum of one per dewatering well, upstream of the main header pipe, to allow for monitoring of water quality from each well location</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>DELIVERY, STORAGE, AND HANDLING</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Deliver equipment, materials, and appurtenances to the site in original unopened containers bearing the manufacturer's name, model, project identification, handling instructions, and related information. Inspect all equipment for damage when received and replace damaged equipment at no additional cost to the Contracting Officer. Store equipment indoors or as recommended by the manufacturer, protected from construction or weather hazards. Follow manufacturers' instructions for extended storage.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
</PRT>
<PRT>
<TTL>PART 3 EXECUTION</TTL><BRK/>
<SPT>
<TTL>GENERAL</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Continuously control water during the entire course of construction, including nights, weekends, holidays, and periods of work stoppages, in accordance with the continuous operation requirements of paragraph 2.3(f). Provide backup systems to maintain control of water until construction has been completed to such an extent that the foundation, structure, or fill will not be damaged by allowing water levels to return to natural elevations.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Perform all construction and backfilling in-the-dry.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Install and operate each dewatering system and demonstrate through prove-out testing (paragraph 3.3.4) that the applicable performance criteria have been achieved prior to commencing construction activities.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Locate dewatering system components where they will not interfere with construction activities, the installation and monitoring of geotechnical instrumentation, temporary or permanent utilities, or the permanent Dry Dock structure and its appurtenances. Coordinate location of equipment with other trades. Rework caused by conflicts is the sole responsibility of the Contractor.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Do not penetrate any temporary or permanent structure dewatering system elements any temporary or permanent structure unless explicitly approved by the Contracting Officer.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Design each dewatering system with redundancy such that the loss of a single dewatering well or well segment does not require shutdown of the entire system and dewatering objectives continue to be met during repair.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="g.">Modify or enhance any dewatering system at no additional cost to the Government if, after installation and while in operation, it does not meet the performance criteria of paragraph 1.5 or adversely affects adjacent properties, existing buildings, structures, utilities, or new construction.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>DEWATERING SYSTEM INSTALLATION AND PROVE-OUT</TTL><BRK/>
<SPT>
<TTL>Installation - General</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Install each dewatering system in accordance with the accepted submittals.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Install dewatering wells using methods that do not disturb or reduce the bearing capacity of adjacent subgrades, foundations, or the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall and Double Wall cofferdam systems.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Provide the services of a representative of the vendor and manufacturer experienced in the installation, adjustment, maintenance, and operation of the equipment to supervise installation, adjustment, and testing. The lead designer or an engineering representative experienced in leading installation of dewatering systems is to be present during system installation, prove-out, and startup.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Well Development</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Following installation, and as soon as practicable, develop each dewatering well by surging with a surge block raised and lowered through the well screen at a speed of approximately 2 feet per second. The surge block is to have a diameter 1 to 2 inches smaller than the internal diameter of the well screen.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Each cycle of surging is to consist of at least 15 up and down trips with the surge block. After each cycle, measure the amount of material deposited in the bottom of the well. Continue surging until the infiltration of sediment through the well screen in any one cycle is less than approximately 2.5 inches deep.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">If the accumulation of material at the bottom of the well exceeds 1 foot at any time during surging, bail the well clean.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Do not damage well components during development activities. Air-lifting is not an acceptable method for well development.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">After development, pump each dewatering well at the design discharge rate for a minimum of 30 minutes and up to 3 hours until water is clear as defined in paragraph (g). If the pumped water is not clear after 3 hours, redevelop the well and restart the process.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Record periodic measurements during pumping of well flow, drawdown, depth of sand in the bottom of the well, and amount of sand in the discharge. Calculate and record specific capacity.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="g.">Develop and pump each dewatering well until the amount of sand in the discharge measures less than 5 parts per million (ppm) fines, as measured with a Roscoe Moss Rossum centrifugal sand content tester or equal.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="h.">Submit <SUB>Well Development Data</SUB> , including method, duration, and results (specific capacity, sand content, clarity of discharge), for Contracting Officer approval.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="i.">Submit as-built drawings and well installation logs for all wells within 7 calendar days of installation. Include borehole diameter, screen type, screen slot size, screen top and bottom elevations, filter gradation and placement, seal and grout details, soil and rock strata encountered, and depths of water loss or increased flow.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Quality Control Testing</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Following installation of each dewatering system, conduct the following testing prior to full-scale system startup:</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Equipment Inspection and Testing - Inspect all installed equipment for proper installation and test to ensure functionality.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Hydrostatic Testing - Complete hydrostatic testing on all installed piping. Propose a testing plan based on the type of piping installed and follow appropriate industry-established testing guidelines.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Equipment Calibration - Calibrate all equipment in accordance with manufacturer requirements and provide <SUB>Calibration Certificates</SUB> to the Contracting Officer.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Pump Testing - Test pumps to ensure correct installation.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Testing of Backup/Redundant Equipment - Test all backup and redundant equipment for functionality.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Complete testing on all electrical components and instruments, including proper power supply, motor rotation, terminations, and grounding.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="g.">If any deficiencies are revealed during testing, correct deficiencies and repeat tests. Document a complete log of each test run with date, time of readings, and total water circulated.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Prove-Out Testing</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Submit a <SUB>Dewatering Systems Prove-Out Plan</SUB> detailing the steps to be completed during the prove-out test and the metrics for a successful test. Obtain Contracting Officer approval of this plan prior to commencement.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Conduct a Prove-Out test prior to full-scale operation of each dewatering system. Perform prove-out testing after the installation of control and monitoring systems but prior to removal of surface water from the cofferdam capture area.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Execute the prove-out test as outlined in the Prove-Out plan, or until steady-state or near-steady-state conditions are established, whichever is longer. The test is to demonstrate a dropping piezometric surface and assist with identifying leaks or defects in the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall or Double Wall cofferdam.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Based on Prove-Out results, determine whether the system as installed is sufficient for full-scale operation or requires additional infrastructure.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">During prove-out, take startup readings on pressure at each wellhead, piezometric readings, pump parameters, pumping flow, totalizers, runtime, and sand content of discharge water. Take detailed notes for modifying standard operating procedures, recording startup conditions, and documenting typical operating conditions.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Monitor water levels in monitoring wells during prove-out to ensure the dewatering well network influences the entire excavation area to the extent where dewatering is demonstrated achievable during full-time operation.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="g.">Immediately replace observation wells, piezometers, level transducers, or other instruments that become inoperable prior to conclusion of prove-out testing.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="h.">Address any deficiencies, inefficiencies, or inadequacies, including:</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="h.(1)">Failure to reduce the piezometric surface.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="h.(2)">Failure to deliver water to the pre-treatment system.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="h.(3)">Wells pumping sand.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="i.">Prepare a <SUB>Prove-Out Testing Results Report</SUB> detailing the effectiveness of each system and any modifications made during the prove-out period. This report should include a detailed narrative of the test, documenting that the system is operating as designed. All data collected during the test, including individual well and system flowrates, water levels, pressures, and other relevant data, This report should be stamped by e P.E. and requires Contracting Officer approval prior to full-scale system startup.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="j.">The Contracting Officer reserves the right to require an additional Prove-Out period if the initial Prove-Out is inadequate or if dewatering system adjustments are required to meet performance requirements.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>DEWATERING SYSTEM OPERATION</TTL><BRK/>
<SPT>
<TTL>General Operation</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Conduct daily inspection and maintenance of all dewatering system components.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Replace or repair damaged components at no additional cost to the Government.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Operate all dewatering systems continuously in accordance with paragraph 2.3(f) to maintain the water levels specified in paragraph 1.5 or until structures have been constructed and backfilled to an extent that they are not at risk from hydrostatic pressure, flotation, or other groundwater-related effects, as verified by the Contracting Officer in accordance with Hold Point No. 2 (paragraph 1.5.8).</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Lower groundwater levels or hydrostatic pressure heads in accordance with the applicable performance criteria for each system prior to excavation and maintain those levels at all times, including through full tidal cycles and during storm events up to the 50-year recurrence interval.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Monitor dewatering system discharge for evidence of fines migration. If loss of ground or fines in the discharge is observed, immediately cease pumping from the affected component, take remedial action, and notify the Contracting Officer.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Monitor dewatering well discharge for settleable solids content on a daily basis for one month after each well installation and weekly thereafter. Wells that pump sand at greater than 5 ppm on a Rossum Sand Tester are not acceptable. Replace sand-pumping wells with revised designs and development methods.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="g.">If any dewatering system is modified during installation or operation, revise and resubmit the <SUB>Dewatering and Groundwater Control Plan</SUB> for acceptance prior to implementing changes. For emergency modifications required to prevent imminent damage, document and submit the modifications within 72 hours.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="h.">If any dewatering system is unable to maintain its performance criteria the Contracting Officer must be notified with one hour of loss of required conditions and the Contractor must remedy the condition.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Operator Requirements</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Assign an operator or group of operators who are designated qualified persons covering operations during required continuous operation in accordance with paragraph 2.3(f). Qualified persons are to have relevant project experience with the operation of similar systems and have completed the field training in paragraph 3.4.3.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">This group constitutes the dewatering team and their primary responsibility is to operate, maintain, and carry out activities for maintaining all systems.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Qualified personnel are responsible for conducting field training of support staff members.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Field Training Requirements</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Conduct training for designated operations, maintenance, and support staff members. The training period is a total of 24 hours of normal working time and is to start after the system is functionally completed.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Field training is to cover each item contained in the O&M manual.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Maintain a record of all personnel that have been trained on site for the duration of the project.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Operations and Maintenance</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Provide operation, maintenance, service, and repairs of all dewatering systems and appurtenances during the life of the contract, including repairing and replacing parts and systems. Provide routine testing of backup power and pumping systems/instruments for functionality.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Develop an O&M manual that includes:</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(1)">Functional description of each system</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(2)">Process flow, instrumentation, equipment arrangement, and electrical drawings</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(3)">Detailed descriptions of HMI controls</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(4)">Standard operating procedures for each piece of equipment</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(5)">System startup/shutdown procedures</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(6)">System setpoints and operating boundaries</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(7)">System troubleshooting procedures</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(8)">Preventive maintenance schedules and procedures</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(9)">Spare parts list</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(10)">Equipment cut sheets</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">The <SUB>Preventive Maintenance Plan and Schedule</SUB> is to include all routine recommended preventive measures for handling contaminants, fouling of well screens and pumps, cleaning of the header system, and handling of waste products.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">All system operators and technicians are to review and sign the acknowledgement form in the O&M manual. Signing is required prior to operating, maintaining, or repairing any system component.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Keep the O&M manual on site at all times and update as system components or operating conditions change. System operators are to sign off to acknowledge they have reviewed each update.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Instrumentation and Equipment Maintenance</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Calibrate instrumentation in accordance with manufacturer recommendations.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Replace instrumentation that fails to comply or is deemed defective immediately.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Replace equipment that proves faulty or significantly inefficient immediately.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Provide backups for critical equipment such as pumps, sensors, gauges, transducers, and flow meters available on site to ensure timely replacement and minimal downtime.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>System Performance Monitoring</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Perform instrumentation and performance monitoring for all dewatering systems, including observation wells, piezometers, settlement monuments, and displacement monitoring of existing and new structures, in accordance with Section <SRF>33 51 43</SRF> , INSTRUMENTATION AND PERFORMANCE MONITORING OF STRUCTURES.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Coordinate all dewatering system designs and operations with the Project instrumentation and monitoring program. Modify dewatering operations if monitoring data indicate that dewatering is causing or threatening to cause settlement, displacement, or damage to existing or new structures, utilities, or marine infrastructure.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Include testing of water level observation monitoring wells in the O&M plan, at a frequency defined by the Contractor and approved by the Contracting Officer, by adding and removing water from the risers to demonstrate and report on proper functioning.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Review system operational data daily and rectify any conditions that do not meet performance requirements.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Maintain detailed system performance logs, copied and uploaded onto a shared database for review by the Contracting Officer.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Inspect equipment in use and on standby for deficiencies on a Contractor-defined schedule. Maintain a routine maintenance schedule and checklist.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>COFFERDAM AND CUTOFF WALL INTERFACE</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Design each dewatering system to account for residual seepage through, beneath, and around the onshore and offshore cutoff systems described in paragraph 1.5.7(d). Include seepage through construction joints, interlocks, panel joints, and localized defects. Perform seepage analysis in accordance with industry accepted methods.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Design dewatering systems to accommodate tidal lag effects and maintain performance criteria throughout the full tidal cycle.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Coordinate the sequence of dewatering system installation and operation with construction of the Upland Concrete Shoring Wall and Double Wall Cofferdam. Do not induce unbalanced pressures or differential heads across any cutoff that exceed the design specifications for that cutoff system.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">If dewatering causes or threatens to cause piping, seepage, erosion, or instability at the soil - cutoff wall interface or the native soil - cofferdam fill interface, immediately reduce pumping rates, take remedial action, and notify the Contracting Officer.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Coordinate dewatering system operation with vibrocompaction and DSM operations within the Double Wall Cofferdam. Do not operate any dewatering system at full drawdown within the Double Wall cofferdam until:</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="e.(1)">Vibrocompaction is complete and excess pore water pressures have dissipated per project specifications.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="e.(2)">DSM columns have achieved a minimum cure age per project specifications and the design unconfined compressive strength has been verified by coring.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Account for the potential extraction of fines from recently placed sand fill during initial dewatering. Adjust pumping rates and monitor discharge turbidity.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>MONITORING AND RECORDING FLOWS</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Monitor and record the instantaneous flow rate and total daily volume for each dewatering well and for each header in each dewatering system.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Obtain and review flow monitoring records daily..</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Monitor dewatering system discharge for evidence of fines extraction. If turbidity exceeds 50 nephelometric turbidity units (NTU) or visible soil particles are observed, cease pumping from the affected component and take corrective action.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Submit discharge flow rate records to the Contracting Officer weekly.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>DISPOSAL OF WATER</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Convey all water discharged from the dewatering systems to the designated pre-treatment system. Water treatment and discharge compliance are specified in Section <SRF>01 57 19</SRF> , TEMPORARY ENVIRONMENTAL CONTROLS.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>CORRECTIVE ACTION</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">If dewatering requirements are not satisfied due to failure of any dewatering system, including loosening of foundation strata, instability of slopes, or damage to permanent or temporary structures, perform all work to reinstate foundation soils and repair damaged structures at no additional cost to the Government.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Immediately repair damages to adjacent facilities caused by dewatering operations, at no additional cost to the Government.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>PROTECTION</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Conduct dewatering operations in a manner that does not cause loss of ground or disturbance to the soil supporting existing or designed foundations, structures, utilities, pavements, or marine infrastructure.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Continuously monitor pump discharge to prevent prolonged extraction of soil particles.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Immediately modify or replace dewatering facilities, procedures, or equipment that cause or threaten to cause damage to new or existing facilities, at no additional cost to the Government.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Prevent uplift of each structure during construction.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>STORM AND TIDAL SURGE CONTINGENCY</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Submit a <SUB>Flood and Storm Contingency Plan</SUB> a minimum of 30 calendar days prior to commencement of dewatering.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Include, at a minimum:</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(1)">Activation of all standby pumps and backup power.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(2)">Securing of all loose equipment and materials within the excavation.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(3)">Staged controlled flooding plan for the excavation if dewatering cannot be maintained.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="b.(4)">Post-event inspection and recovery procedures.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Do not cease dewatering during a storm event unless continued operation poses an imminent threat to life safety or the Contracting Officer directs cessation.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>SEISMIC DESIGN PROVISIONS</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Design all installed construction dewatering equipment, including discharge conveyance infrastructure, to meet all project seismic requirements.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>COFFERDAM CAPTURE AREA AND DD3 OPERATIONS</TTL><BRK/>
<SPT>
<TTL>Installation</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Provide the services of a vendor or engineer experienced in the installation and operation of pumping equipment to supervise installation, adjustment, and testing. The lead designer or an engineering representative experienced in performing installation of pumping systems is to be on site during installation.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Operations</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>After installation is complete, the pumping system is to remove water from within the Cofferdam Capture Area and DD3 area to allow construction activities to continue. The pumping system is to extract sufficient flow to dewater the area within the timeline specified by the Contract documents. Convey water in accordance with Section <SRF>01 57 19</SRF> , TEMPORARY ENVIRONMENTAL CONTROLS.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>DECOMMISSIONING</TTL><BRK/>
<SPT>
<TTL>General</TTL><BRK/>
<OLG>
<OLI ITEM="a.">Submit separate Decommissioning Plans for each of the following:</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="a.(1)">Dry Dock Mass Excavation Dewatering System (including transition to permanent Pressure Relief System)</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="a.(2)">Temporary Cofferdam Interior Level Control System</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI LEVEL="2" ITEM="a.(3)">Upland Concrete Shoring Wall Dewatering System</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="b.">Obtain Contracting Officer approval of these plans prior to commencement of decommissioning work.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="c.">Maintain each dewatering system until completion of work within the designated excavation area.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="d.">Remove and backfill or abandon dewatering elements when no longer required, using methods approved by the Contracting Officer in the Decommissioning Plans.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="e.">Dewatering wells advanced through permanent surfaces (e.g., concrete, ground improvement) that cannot be completely removed are to be abandoned in place using industry-accepted methods. Outline well abandonment methods in the decommissioning plan for Contracting Officer approval.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="f.">Abandon all wells in accordance with applicable Washington Administrative Code well abandonment regulations and permit conditions.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="g.">Backfill all excavations made for dewatering operations in accordance with Section <SRF>31 23 00.00 20</SRF> EXCAVATION AND FILL.</OLI><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<OLI ITEM="h.">Following completion of decommissioning and removal of dewatering system components, submit a closeout report documenting the completed decommissioning activities to the Contracting Officer. This report will include a narrative of the decommissioning activities including a description of how each well and component was removed. This report must certify that all codes and permits have been followed and must be stamped by a P.E.</OLI><BRK/>
</OLG>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Transition Between Dry Dock Mass Excavation Dewatering System and Permanent Pressure Relief System</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Include in the Dry Dock Mass Excavation Dewatering System Decommissioning Plan a transition plan from Dry Dock Mass Excavation Dewatering System operations to permanent Pressure Relief System operations (Section <SRF>33 26 01</SRF> , PRESSURE RELIEF SYSTEM). The Dry Dock Mass Excavation Dewatering System will not be decommissioned until the Pressure Relief System is fully operational and its performance as been accepted by the Contracting Officer.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
<SPT>
<TTL>Removal of Cofferdam and DD3 Capture System Components</TTL><BRK/>
<TXT>Following completion of the water removal activities from the Cofferdam and DD3 capture area, remove all pumps, piping, appurtenances, tanks, and other equipment associated with those dewatering activities.</TXT><BRK/>
<BRK/>
</SPT>
</SPT>
</PRT>
</SEC>